= Empirical evidence — Technical =

== Observation reports ==
Empirical evidence is often represented through observation reports, measurements, datasets, traces, images, or instrument records.

Logical empiricists attempted to analyze theory testing through an observation/theory distinction, but later philosophy emphasized that observation can depend on:
* background theory;
* instrument design;
* classification;
* calibration;
* statistical preprocessing;
* model-based reconstruction.

== Data models ==
Raw instrument output often undergoes several transformations:

<pre>
target interaction
→ signal
→ instrument indication
→ calibration / preprocessing
→ data product
→ statistical/model inference
→ evidential claim
</pre>

Each stage can introduce assumptions and [[Uncertainty/Technical|uncertainty]].

== Experiment and intervention ==
Experimental manipulation can support causal identification when treatment assignment and control structures block alternative causal pathways.

Randomization helps balance confounders in expectation, but experimental validity still depends on:
* compliance;
* measurement;
* attrition;
* interference;
* selection;
* analysis choices.

== Observational inference ==
When intervention is impossible, empirical causal evidence may use:
* longitudinal design;
* natural experiments;
* instrumental variables;
* regression discontinuity;
* matching/adjustment;
* causal graphs;
* sensitivity analysis.

These methods identify causal effects only under stated assumptions.

== Theory-ladenness ==
Theory-ladenness does not imply unrestricted relativism.

Robust empirical practice can use:
* multiple instruments;
* independent calibrations;
* blinded analysis;
* pre-specified pipelines;
* cross-validation;
* replication;
* predictions in new regimes.

Convergence under diverse methods can reduce dependence on one representational assumption.

